          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Grothendieck-Teichmüller tower
            construction involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
            outlined in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Esquisse+d%27un+programme"} Esquisse d'un programme
            and then developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Drinfel%27d"} Vladimir Drinfel'd
            and others. It involves the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Teichm%C3%BCller+groupoids"} Teichmüller groupoids
            which are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoids"} fundamental groupoids
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curves"} curves
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            points removed. It is a basis for the definition of the
            %em Grothendieck-Teichmüller group
            which is by the definition inertia-preserving
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            of the Grothendieck-Teichmüller tower.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p …
          %h3#grothendieckteichmller_lie_algebra Grothendieck-Teichmüller Lie algebra
          %p GT Lie algebra appears as the tangent Lie algebra to the GT group.
          %p Drinfel’d defined it more explicitly as follows.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔩𝔦𝔢
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{lie}_n
            be the degree completion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+Lie+algebra"} free Lie algebra
            on
            = succeed "-generators" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            of characteristic
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔡𝔢𝔯
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{der}_n
            be the space of
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔩𝔦𝔢
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔩𝔦𝔢
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathfrak{lie}_n\to
                    \\mathfrak{lie}_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔡𝔢𝔯
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\in\mathfrak{der}_n
            is
            %strong tangential
            if there exist
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔩𝔦𝔢
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i\in\mathfrak{lie}_n
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=1,\ldots,n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u(x_i)=[x_i,a_i]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            is determined by elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1,\ldots,a_n
            and is below denoted by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_1,\ldots,a_n)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔱𝔡𝔢𝔯
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔡𝔢𝔯
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{tder}_n\subset\mathfrak{der}_n
            %strong GT Lie algebra
            is the subspace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤𝔯𝔱
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔱𝔡𝔢𝔯
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{grt}\subset\mathfrak{tder}_2
            consisting of the tangential derivations of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,\psi)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi for
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi all
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\psi(x,y)=-\psi(y,x),\,\,\,for\,\,\,all\,\,\,x,y,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi whenever
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\psi(x,y)+\psi(y,z)+\psi(z,x) = 0,\,\,\,whenever\,\,\,\,x+y+z=0,
          %p and the pentagon-type identity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 34
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 12
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 23
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 23
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\psi(t^{1,2},t^{2,34})+\psi(t^{12,3},t^{3,4})=
                  \\psi(t^{2,3},t^{3,4})+\psi(t^{1,23},t^{23,4})+\psi(t^{1,2},t^{2,3}),
          %p
            hold, and which is equipped with the
            %strong Ihara Lie bracket
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi Ihara
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\psi_1,\psi_2]_{Ihara} = (0,\psi_1)(\psi_2)-(0,\psi_2)(\psi_1)+[\psi_1,\psi_2].
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_drinfeld_associators Relation to Drinfeld associators
          %p
            The GT group acts freely on the set of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Drinfeld+associators"} Drinfeld associators
          %h3#relation_to_the_absolute_galois_group_of_the_rational_numbers Relation to the absolute Galois group of the rational numbers
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Drinfeld, Ihara, Deligne)
            %p
              There is an inclusion of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+Galois+group"} absolute Galois group
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              into the Grothendieck-Teichmüller group (recalled e.g.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Stix04"} Stix 04, theorem 6
          %h3#relation_to_the_motivic_galois_group Relation to the motivic Galois group
          %p
            The Grothendieck-Teichmüller group is supposed to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic Galois group
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Drinfeld91"} Drinfeld 91
          %h3#RelationToTheGraphComplex Relation to the graph complex
          %p
            The Grothendieck-Teichmüller Lie algebra is isomorphic to the 0th cohomology of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+complex"} graph complex
            (
            %a{:href => "#Willwacher10"}> Willwacher 10
            ).
          %h3#relation_to_deformation_quantization_and_the_cosmic_galois_group Relation to deformation quantization and the cosmic Galois group
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            predicted that the GT group is closely related to the absolute Galois group.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            later conjectured its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            on certain spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            and outlined its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} motivic
            aspects.
          %p
            This was later proven by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vasily+Dolgushev"} Vasily Dolgushev
            %em
              %a{:href => "deformation+quantization#MotivicGaloisGroup"} formal deformation quantization – Motivic Galois group action on the space of quantizations
            for details and pointers.
          %p
            For more see also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"} cosmic Galois group
            for more on this.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+symplectic+category"}> stable symplectic category
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"}> motivic Galois group
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"} cosmic Galois group
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The Grothendieck-Teichmüller group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GRT
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GRT
            was originally introduced in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Drinfel%27d"}> Vladimir Drinfel'd
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  On quasitriangular quasi-Hopf algebras and on a group that is closely connected with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Gal
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %mi ℚ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi ℚ
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal(\overline{\mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Q}})
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://mi.mathnet.ru/aa199"} abs
          %p inspired by
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sketch of a program
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#Fresse18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benoit+Fresse"}> Benoit Fresse
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Little discs operads, graph complexes and Grothendieck–Teichmüller groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haynes+Miller"} Haynes Miller
              (ed.)
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Homotopy+Theory"} Handbook of Homotopy Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.12536"}> arXiv:1811.12536
              )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leila+Schneps"}> Leila Schneps
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Lochak"} Pierre Lochak
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric Galois actions
                = succeed ")\u2013" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1017%2FCBO9780511666124"} doi
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Grothendieck-Teichmueller group GT: a survey
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.jussieu.fr/~leila/SchnepsGT.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Operads and motives in deformation quantization
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9904055"} math.QA/9904055
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1007555725247"} doi
            %li#FresseHOGTG
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benoit+Fresse"}> Benoit Fresse
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy of Operads and Grothendieck-Teichmüller Groups (Volumes 1 &amp; 2)
                %strong 217
                (2017) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-217/"}> ISBN:978-1-4704-3480-9
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.univ-lille1.fr/~fresse/OperadHomotopyBook/"} website
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.648.9110&rep=rep1&type=pdf"} pdf (draft of Part I)
          %blockquote
            %p The second purpose of the book is to explain, from a homotopical viewpoint, a deep relationship between operads and Grothendieck-Teichmüller groups. This connection, which has been foreseen by M. Kontsevich (from researches on the deformation quantization process in mathematical physics), gives a new approach to understanding internal symmetries of structures occurring in various constructions of algebra and topology. In the book, we set up the background required by an in-depth study of this subject, and we make precise the interpretation of the Grothendieck-Teichmüller group in terms of the homotopy of operads. The book is actually organized for this ultimate objective, which readers can take either as a main motivation or as a leading example to learn about general theories.
          %p Videos from a seminar at the Newton Institute:
          %ul
            %li
              Newton Institute Programme Jan-April 2013: Grothendieck-Teichmüller Groups, Deformation and Operads,
              %a{:href => "https://www.newton.ac.uk/event/gdo/seminars"} seminars
              (some with videos)
          %p
            Relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+groups"} automorphism groups
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+completion"} profinite completion
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+groups"} braid groups
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Lochak"}> Pierre Lochak
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leila+Schneps"} Leila Schneps
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Grothendieck-Teichmüller group and automorphisms of braid groups
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Grothendieck Theory of Dessins d’Enfant
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~leila.schneps/Fls.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511569302"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511569302
          %p The Drinfeld conjecture is stated in
          %ul
            %li#Drinfeld91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Drinfel%27d"}> Vladimir Drinfel'd
              ,
              %em
                On quasi-triangular quasi-Hopf algebras and a group closely related with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Gal
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %mi ℚ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi ℚ
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal(\overline{\mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Q}})
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Stix04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Stix"}> Jakob Stix
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Grothendieck-Teichmüller group and Galois theory of the rational numbers
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/StiXGaloisAndGT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anton+Alekseev"}> Anton Alekseev
                , Charles Torossian,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Kashiwara-Vergne conjecture and Drinfeld’s associators
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0802.4300"} arxiv/0802.4300
          %h3#relation_to_graph_complexes Relation to graph complexes
          %p
            Relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+complexes"} graph complexes
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Graphs
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^0\big( Graphs_0(\mathbb{R}^2) \big)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck-Teichm%C3%BCller+group"} Grothendieck-Teichmüller group
          %ul
            %li#Willwacher10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Willwacher"}> Thomas Willwacher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em M. Kontsevich’s graph complex and the Grothendieck-Teichmueller Lie algebra
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1009.1654"} arxiv:1009.1654
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+Kontsevich"}> M. Kontsevich
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Derived Grothendieck–Teichmüller group and graph complexes [after T. Willwacher]
                %a{:href => "https://www.bourbaki.fr/TEXTES/1126.pdf"} pdf
            %li#DolgushevRogers12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vasily+Dolgushev"}> Vasily Dolgushev
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christopher+Rogers"} Christopher Rogers
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Notes on algebraic operads, graph complexes, and Willwacher’s construction
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.2937"} arXiv:1202.2937
            %li
              %p
                V. A. Dolgushev, C. L. Rogers, T. H. Willwacher,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Kontsevich’s graph complex, GRT, and the deformation complex of the sheaf of polyvector fields
                = succeed ":3" do
                  %strong 182
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2015.182.3.2"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vasily+Dolgushev"}> Vasily Dolgushev
                ,
                %em A manifestation of the Grothendieck-Teichmueller group in geometry
                (
                %a{:href => "https://math.temple.edu/~vald/AMSslides.pdf"}> slides pdf
                )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Kevin Morand,
              %em A note on multi-oriented graph complexes and deformation quantization of Lie bialgebroids
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.07593"}> arXiv:2102.07593
              )
